course: geography
lesson: 23
title: Weather, storms and the changing climate
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Weather is what the sky is doing right now: a shower on the way to school, a windy afternoon. Climate is the long average of weather over thirty years or more, the way your mood today differs from your whole personality.
explain: Storms are moving heat. A thunderstorm grows where warm, wet air shoots upwards into a tall cloud; a hurricane is a whole spinning engine of storms fed by warm tropical sea, with a calm eye in the middle and the fiercest wind just around it.
explain: Some gases in the air, like carbon dioxide, act like a blanket and keep Earth warm enough to live on. Since the 1800s people have burned so much coal, oil and gas that the blanket has thickened and Earth has warmed by about 1.3 degrees on average.
explain: Climate has changed before, more slowly: in the Little Ice Age the Thames in London froze hard enough for frost fairs on the ice several times in the 1600s and 1700s.
explain: Europa Universalis 5 ends in 1836, just as coal and steam were starting the warming of today, and in the game a winter is just a season on the map. Real people of those centuries lived through colder winters, failed harvests and hurricanes that sank many Spanish treasure ships.
fact: Weather is what the air is doing today, while climate is the average pattern of weather over about 30 years or more.
fact: In Britain much rain comes from fronts, where a mass of warm air meets a mass of cold air and the warm air is pushed upwards; TV weather maps draw fronts as lines with triangles and half-circles.
fact: Hurricanes, typhoons and cyclones are the same kind of storm, a tropical cyclone, with different names in different oceans: hurricanes in the Atlantic, typhoons near Japan and China, and cyclones in the Indian Ocean.
fact: A tropical cyclone has a calm, often clear centre called the eye, surrounded by a wall of the strongest winds; as the eye passes over, the storm goes quiet for a while, then the other side of the wall hits.
fact: A thunderstorm forms when warm, moist air rises fast and builds a tall cumulonimbus cloud, where ice and water bump together and build up electric charge.
fact: Light travels much faster than sound, so you see lightning before you hear thunder, and each 3 seconds between them means about 1 kilometre.
fact: Gases such as carbon dioxide and methane trap some of the heat Earth gives off, keeping the planet warm enough for life; without them Earth would average about minus 18 degrees Celsius; this is called the greenhouse effect.
fact: Since the 1800s Earth has warmed by about 1.3 degrees Celsius on average, mainly because people burn coal, oil and gas.
fact: From roughly the 1300s to the 1800s, much of the northern hemisphere was somewhat cooler than before or after, a period called the Little Ice Age.
fact: Sea level has risen by more than 20 centimetres since 1900, and it is now rising faster, which puts low places such as the Netherlands, Bangladesh and islands like Tuvalu most at risk; melting ice on land raises the sea, but floating sea ice already pushes aside its own weight of water, so its melting barely does.
fact: A warmer climate makes heatwaves more common, dries the ground faster and, because warm air holds more water, can make heavy downpours heavier, and in 2022 Britain recorded over 40 degrees Celsius for the first time.
fact: El Niño is a pattern every few years in which the eastern tropical Pacific becomes unusually warm, changing weather around the world, for example bringing drought to Australia and floods to Peru.
exercise: What is the difference between weather and climate? | Weather is today's air; climate is the average over about 30 years | Weather is the average over 30 years; climate is what the sky does today | They are two words for exactly the same thing, used in different countries | Weather is about rain; climate is only about temperature
exercise: What is a front? | A place where a warm air mass meets a cold one | The bright edge of a cloud that faces the Sun | A strong wind from the sea | The calm middle of a storm
exercise: What is a hurricane called near Japan and China? | A typhoon | A tornado | A monsoon | A blizzard
exercise: Are hurricanes, typhoons and cyclones different kinds of storm? | No, they are all tropical cyclones with local names | Yes, typhoons are much bigger and stronger than hurricanes | Yes, cyclones form only over land, never over the sea | Yes, hurricanes have no eye
exercise: What is the eye of a tropical cyclone? | A calm, often clear centre | The spot with the strongest winds | The place where lightning starts | The front edge where the rain begins
exercise: Where are the strongest winds in a tropical cyclone? | In the wall around the eye | Right in the middle of the eye | Far out at the edge of the storm | Above the clouds, never at the ground
exercise: What kind of cloud makes a thunderstorm? | A tall cumulonimbus cloud | A thin, wispy high cloud | A flat grey fog | A small white cloud
exercise: What builds up electric charge in a thundercloud? | Ice and water bumping together | Sunlight hitting the top of the cloud | Wind rubbing against mountains | Birds flying through the cloud
exercise: What do gases like carbon dioxide and methane do? | Trap some of Earth's heat | Block all sunlight from reaching us | Make holes that let heat escape | Turn the rain into snow and hail
exercise: By about how much has Earth warmed since the 1800s? | About 1.3 degrees Celsius | About 13 degrees Celsius | About 0.01 degrees Celsius | It has not warmed at all
exercise: What is the main cause of the warming since the 1800s? | People burning coal, oil and gas | The Sun moving closer to Earth | Volcanoes erupting more often | The Moon reflecting more light
exercise: What was the Little Ice Age? | A cooler time from about the 1300s to the 1800s | The last time ice covered the whole Earth | A single winter in 1900 when Britain froze | The time of the woolly mammoths
exercise: By how much has the sea risen since 1900? | By more than 20 centimetres | By about 20 metres since 1900 | By about 2 millimetres in all | It has gone down, not up, since 1900
exercise: What does a warmer climate do to heatwaves? | Makes them more common | Makes them stop altogether | Moves them all to the poles | Makes them shorter but colder
exercise: What is El Niño? | An unusually warm eastern tropical Pacific every few years | A cold wind that blows down over Spain and Portugal each winter | A hurricane that hits Mexico every summer | A warm current that flows past Britain
exercise: What did Britain record for the first time in 2022? | Over 40 degrees Celsius | A hurricane on the Thames | Snow lying on the ground in July | A year with no rain at all
test: It snowed loads today so global warming is fake lol | One snowy day is weather; climate is the long average | Yes, a big snowfall proves the planet is cooling down again | Yes, warming only means no snow ever, anywhere | No, snow is caused by warming every day
test: The storm's gone quiet and the sky's blue, can we go out and look?? | No, the eye is passing and the other wall will hit soon | Yes, a quiet sky means the storm is over | Yes, the eye is the safest place to be outside | No, but only because it is getting dark
test: You count 9 seconds between flash and bang. About how far off is the storm? | About 3 kilometres | About 9 kilometres | About 30 kilometres | Right overhead
test: Dad says the greenhouse effect is pure evil and we'd be better off without it. Put him right. | Without it Earth would be about minus 18 degrees and frozen | He is right, it only harms the planet and does nothing good for us | It has nothing at all to do with Earth's temperature | Without it Earth would be much hotter, like the planet Venus
test: Why could people hold a fair on the frozen Thames in the 1600s but not today? | Winters were colder in the Little Ice Age and the climate has warmed | The Thames was much deeper back then, and deep water freezes more easily | People then had special ice machines | The river was saltier and froze faster
test: A warm year in the Pacific off Peru, and Australia gets a drought. How can that be? | El Niño changes winds and rain far around the world | Australia is right next to Peru | The warm water flows to Australia and dries it | It is only a coincidence and never happens again
test: How can a warmer world have both more droughts and heavier downpours? | Warm air dries the ground faster and also holds more water to rain out | Warming only causes floods, never droughts; the droughts come from elsewhere | Droughts and floods both come from the world cooling down | Warming moves all the rain away to the poles
test: Melting ice on land raises the sea, but melting floating sea ice barely does. Why? | Floating ice already pushes aside its own weight of water | Sea ice is made of fresh water, which simply vanishes when it melts | Land ice is much colder and bigger per drop | Sea ice melts into the air, not into the sea
test: In the game winter is just a season. What did real people of the Little Ice Age face? | Colder winters and failed harvests in many years | Ice covering the whole of Europe all year, so nobody could farm | Exactly the same weather as today | Hot summers every single year
test: Why do the weather maps on TV show lines with triangles and half-circles? | They mark fronts, where warm and cold air meet | They mark the borders between the counties | They show where the trains run | They show where lightning is going to strike today
test: Which places are especially at risk as the sea rises? | The Netherlands, Bangladesh and islands like Tuvalu | High mountain valleys such as those in the Alps | Deserts far from the sea, like the middle of the Sahara | Every place on Earth exactly equally
exam: Identify the principal cause of the elevation of the mean global temperature observed since the nineteenth century. | The combustion by mankind of coal, oil and natural gas | A diminution in the distance between the Earth and the Sun | An increase in the frequency of volcanic eruptions | The reflection of additional light by the Moon
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the distinction between weather and climate? | Weather denotes present conditions; climate denotes their average over some thirty years | Climate denotes present conditions; weather denotes their average over several centuries | Weather pertains solely to precipitation; climate solely to temperature | The two terms are entirely synonymous in scientific usage
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning hurricanes, typhoons and cyclones. | They constitute a single phenomenon, the tropical cyclone, named variously by ocean | They constitute three distinct phenomena of differing structure, size and origin | Typhoons alone possess a calm central eye, which hurricanes and cyclones invariably lack | Cyclones are confined exclusively to continental interiors
exam: It is given that during the passage of a tropical cyclone the wind abruptly abates and the sky clears. Ascertain the most probable explanation. | The eye is passing, and the opposite eyewall will shortly arrive | The cyclone has dissipated entirely and the danger is past | The cyclone has reversed its course and departed | A front of cold air has extinguished the storm
